WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.680
What's up? Welcome to Models and Agents, Episode 105. It's July 8th, 2026. Lots of news to walk through. Let's start with what actually matters.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:18.160
Open AI's next frontier model arrives Thursday, while a 2.7 trillion parameter Chinese model, ready for open release.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:24.000
Sam Altman confirmed that GPT 5.6 saw launches this week.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:32.680
Minimax plans a 2.7 trillion parameter M3 Pro model for Q3, open sourcing focused on complex reasoning.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:44.420
Simon Willison's Claude Fable 5 review of SkeetQ EPA-Typhen Udels 4.0 surfaced four additional blockers plus an upgrade guide agents can consume directly.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:49.360
Builders should watch release timing and test the new upgrade tooling this week.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:01:00.740
Sam Altman announced GPT 5.6 saw while launch Thursday. The release follows limited preview testing and targets global rollout after initial access.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:09.160
It continues open AI's pattern of iterative frontier updates that trade incremental capability gains against inference cost.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:17.400
Developers building production agents or long context applications should prepare integration tests once the model hits the API.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:27.040
The announcement leaves open questions about context length, pricing tiers, and whether it narrows the gap with open weight competitors on reasoning benchmarks.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:33.620
Watch for the exact release notes and any new tool calling improvements that could affect agent reliability.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:40.960
Minimax is preparing a 2.7 trillion parameter model internally codenamed M3 Pro.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:48.980
It will be released and open sourced as early as Q3 with claimed gains on complex reasoning and multi-step tasks.

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:55.280
The new model dwarfs the company's current 428 billion parameter M3 flagship.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:02:01.320
Builders tracking open weight scaling should monitor hugging face for the weights once they drop.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:09.460
This fits the ongoing arc for open weight models as they continue narrowing the performance gap with closed frontier systems.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:16.800
Token AI released two open source hieroglyph translation models built on Quinn 3.5.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:29.480
Horus Hyro 9b and the 4b mini variant support text, image, and video input across approximately 150 languages with a 512,000 context window.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:43.800
They report 79% on MMLU Pro, 63% on live code bench, and 84% on human eval, while adding the first large scale multi-modal hieroglyph capability.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:48.580
The models are available on hugging face with neural node framework support.

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:59.740
Community benchmarks show MIMO V2.5 outperforming deep-seek V4 Flash on several coding harnesses including Codex, Omipy, and Hermes.

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:07.620
Terminal bench V2.0 scores reached 55% with MIMO versus under 50% for the compared models.

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:15.060
Users report stronger performance on real world complex problem solving despite similar aggregate benchmark numbers.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:27.640
Simon Willison reports Claude Fable 5 demonstrated strong taste doing SkiKman4.utles 4.0 code review, catching API design issues the author had missed.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:32.960
The model was given a final release prompt and identified four additional blockers.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:40.040
This continues the pattern of frontier models serving as high signal code reviewers for open source maintainers.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:48.980
A new study tested four open models in an iterated split or steel game against a fixed GPT 4.1 mini virtual human.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:57.980
The models were Ministral 33B, 5414B, Gemma 312B, and Gemma 4E4B.

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:05.880
Pro-social and principled personas produced the most consistent cooperation while analytical personas were likely to exploit.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:12.480
The work provides a baseline for future embodied VR studies of trust and strategic behavior.

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:25.700
Researchers trained search routing policies on Gemma E2B and Q1 3.5 to 4B using counterfactual supervision that compares no search versus forced search outcomes.

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:41.740
MacroF1 on Oracle eligible examples rose from 0.708 to 0.8235 for Gemma and from 0.7053 to 0.8365 for Quinn.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:48.340
The policies reduce model specific failures such as unnecessary search calls or missed retrieval.

00:04:48.340 --> 00:05:03.680
A workload aware study compared Kavi, TurboQuant, SnapKavi, and CAM on Lama 3.1 to 8B Instruct and Meastral 7B Instruct V0.3 across long bench tasks.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:07.900
Compression ratio alone poorly predicted end-to-end performance.

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:14.880
Kavi4 offered the most stable quality while SnapKavi delivered the strongest long context throughput.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:20.740
The results argue for workload aware selection rather than one size fits all compression.

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:35.740
Removing the peer speaker from conformity prompts still caused harmful revision in 66.5% of initially correct answers across 6 open-weight LLMs versus 10.3% for a plain re-ask.

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:43.800
The finding establishes a speaker-free floor that existing benchmarks must measure before attributing changes to social influence.

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:52.940
A detailed upgrade guide for the slightly backwards incompatible SQL-utals 4.0 release is now available.

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:59.100
The guide is designed to be fed directly to coding agents so they can apply the changes automatically.

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:06.640
Maintainers can also read it manually at SQL-dyte-hiphon-utals.dataset.io

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:15.860
Clawed Fable 5 identified 4 additional SQL-dyte-hiphon-utals 4.0 release blockers during a final review pass.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:20.680
The prompt and findings are documented on SimonWillison.net.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:33.380
A new 200-document-buying-leg government form comprehension benchmark tests ML-LMs on layout analysis and key information extraction across 26 fine-grained entity types.

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:44.400
Zero-shot and chain-of-thought evaluations of chat GPT, Gemini, Clawed, Quen, and Kimi series reveal current limitations in localizing granular entities.

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:58.740
The data set is released on hugging face. A three-stage retrieval, re-rank, reasoned pipeline built from 4B open-weight models matches Frontier KOL search accuracy at roughly 35 times lower token cost.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:11.880
The system reaches 94.1% Pat 5 on an 11-query time-marketing benchmark while serving a 50-KOL query in approximately 20 seconds on 1A100.

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:23.620
OK, let's pop the hood on span level uncertainty quantification. Everyone talks about uncertainty estimation as if token level or sequence level scores are sufficient.

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:30.240
In practice, both granularities create real problems for error localization and self-refinement.

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:37.500
Token scores lack semantic coherence while sequence scores cannot point to the specific span that went wrong.

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:49.600
Span U solves this by distilling multi-sample verification signals into a single forward pass that detects coherent spans and models their uncertainty with a mixture of beta distributions.

00:07:49.600 --> 00:08:03.340
The DETR-style decoder achieves 0.910F1 on span detection, 39% above the best heuristic baseline, while running 10 to 20 times faster than sampling methods.

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:18.660
The approach generalizes across five different LLM backbones. When you need precise error localization for agent self-correction rather than just a scalar confidence number, span level methods are worth the added decoder complexity.

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:24.140
Otherwise, the simpler sequence score still suffices for many filtering use cases.

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:33.780
Feed Simon Willison's Ski White-Yudels 4.0 upgrade guide into your coding agent and let it apply the changes on a test branch.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:43.280
Benchmark MIMO V2.5 against deep-seek V4 Flash on your own complex coding tasks using the terminal bench harness.

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:52.040
Test the new Horus Hero 9B or 4B models on any multi-modal hero glyph or ancient script translation work.

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:59.660
Run the Influe match cascade locally if you need low-cost KOL or expert matching for tie-language campaigns.

